    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots into the codebase's existing conventions cleanly:\n\n- **`OwnerRiskFlag` is the right unit.** It mirrors `OwnerSegment` exactly \u2014 a `public final` \"single source of truth\" class with a private constructor, one static derivation method, and a javadoc that enumerates the rule and notes it's derived at read time. The existing code already distinguishes trivial inline derivations in the mapper (`displayName`, `contactPreference`) from rule-bearing ones that get a dedicated class (`OwnerSegment`, `OwnerAgeBand`). A three-condition risk rule belongs in the latter category, so a dedicated class rather than an inline mapper default is the natural choice here.\n\n- **`isDisposableAdjacent` is in the right place.** It lives in `OwnerEmail` beside the existing `isDisposable`, reusing `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` and the shared `baseLabel` helper rather than re-deriving domain-parsing logic elsewhere. The new `DISPOSABLE_BASES` set is computed from `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` so the two can't drift, and `baseLabel` is factored out and used by both consumers \u2014 no duplication.\n\n- **Composition is coherent.** `OwnerRiskFlag.riskFlag` reads the two stored boolean flags directly and delegates only the email-domain judgment to `OwnerEmail`. That split is appropriate: the flags are plain stored state, while the disposable-adjacency check is email-domain logic that rightly stays encapsulated in `OwnerEmail`.\n\n- **Wiring and spec follow the pattern.** The mapper adds a `@Named(\"riskFlag\")` default plus a `@Mapping(... qualifiedByName = \"riskFlag\")` line identical in shape to its siblings, and the import is placed in the existing alphabetical-ish owner-function block. The OpenAPI entry matches the style of the surrounding derived `readOnly` fields.\n\nNothing is becoming a catch-all, nothing is duplicated, and a maintainer would find this a natural extension of the owner-derivation family.",
